WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:05.200
Modules are the individual elements that make up an Unreal code base

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.199
They're the kind of building blocks that you can stitch everything together from the code side to make an Unreal project

3
00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:14.199
There's a couple of reasons why you might want to introduce a module

4
00:00:14.199 --> 00:00:19.199
One of them is if you have components of code that you only want introduced in particular targets

5
00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:26.199
Such as only in a shipping version of the game, only in the editor, only on a client, only on a server, etc

6
00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:29.199
And that's for whole sections of code

7
00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:35.200
The other reason might be that your project is so large that you start to get really long link times

8
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.200
So modules are a way to break that up to say not all of this code needs to link to all of this other code

9
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.200
You can kind of break it up in that way

10
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.200
And the last point is that modules make your code more reusable

11
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:51.200
It's a kind of way to chop up a section of functionality that you could then port to another project very easily

12
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.200
Because you know it's relatively self-contained

13
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.740
So what actually is in an Unreal project?

14
00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.740
The project at the very least consists of a single module which is known as the primary gameplay module

15
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.739
And is essentially your entry point for your XE

16
00:01:05.739 --> 00:01:12.739
And that primary gameplay module can then link to the other modules it needs depending on what kind of functionality it wants

17
00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.739
It's important to highlight as well that when you're building for editor

18
00:01:15.739 --> 00:01:19.739
Each of the modules that your project has are built as individual DLLs

19
00:01:20.739 --> 00:01:22.739
Whereas when you're doing a shipping version of the game

20
00:01:22.739 --> 00:01:27.739
Each of those modules will get combined into one gigantic XE

21
00:01:27.739 --> 00:01:30.739
So if I were to just quickly demonstrate this

22
00:01:30.739 --> 00:01:33.739
We can see here, this is the example project that I've made

23
00:01:33.739 --> 00:01:37.739
And if I went into our binaries, Win64

24
00:01:37.739 --> 00:01:43.739
We can see here we've got an individual DLL for our example 5.5 project

25
00:01:43.739 --> 00:01:46.739
And our example 5.5 project editor

26
00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:52.739
And the reason for that is I've gone ahead and just made an example little secondary module here

27
00:01:52.739 --> 00:01:54.840
For our editor project

28
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.840
I'll go into that in a little bit more detail later

29
00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:08.219
But just to kind of highlight that those things are made as separate DLLs when you're doing an editor build

30
00:02:08.219 --> 00:02:13.219
So if we kind of think about a way of structuring an example here

31
00:02:13.219 --> 00:02:17.219
A slightly contrived example might be that we have an FPS game

32
00:02:17.219 --> 00:02:21.219
And we decide we want to break this up into modules to make it more reusable

33
00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:24.219
So we could have a guns module and then a grenades module

34
00:02:24.219 --> 00:02:28.219
And both of these could then rely on the projectiles module

35
00:02:28.219 --> 00:02:31.219
The advantage of this is it means that we can take guns, we can take grenades

36
00:02:31.219 --> 00:02:36.219
And we can take projectiles all independently of each other and use them in other projects

37
00:02:36.219 --> 00:02:39.219
So our guns would then need to include our projectiles

38
00:02:39.219 --> 00:02:44.219
Because both guns and grenades rely on this projectiles module

39
00:02:44.219 --> 00:02:46.219
So they would both need to rely on it

40
00:02:46.219 --> 00:02:48.219
But it just makes the whole thing more portable

41
00:02:48.219 --> 00:02:50.219
We can take projectiles without the other two

42
00:02:50.219 --> 00:02:53.219
If we wanted to bring guns alone we would need to bring projectiles with it

43
00:02:53.219 --> 00:02:55.219
Because it depends on it

44
00:02:55.219 --> 00:02:58.219
But it just means everything can be kind of chopped and moved around

45
00:02:58.219 --> 00:03:03.219
The one thing to highlight here is if we kind of look at the visual flow

46
00:03:03.219 --> 00:03:06.219
We can see everything is coming out of our primary game module

47
00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:08.219
FPS game being our primary game module

48
00:03:08.219 --> 00:03:12.219
And we need to be a little bit careful about circularity of code here

49
00:03:12.219 --> 00:03:15.219
So we want to avoid circular references here

50
00:03:15.219 --> 00:03:18.219
So we should be very careful about modules linking back up this chain

51
00:03:18.219 --> 00:03:23.219
Guns and grenades shouldn't be linking back to FPS game DLL if they really can't help it

52
00:03:23.219 --> 00:03:26.219
And projectiles shouldn't be linking back to guns

53
00:03:26.219 --> 00:03:30.219
Because it massively increases the chance that you're going to get circular references

54
00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:33.219
Especially as a kind of general working practice

55
00:03:33.219 --> 00:03:36.219
Very few if anything should be linking back to our primary game module

56
00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:40.219
We should really be thinking of our primary game module as the end of chain

57
00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:46.219
An end of that inclusion chain and nothing really should be including that

58
00:03:46.219 --> 00:03:51.219
The one exception I can think of is sometimes when you're using an editor module for your game

59
00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:54.219
That's possibly a case where you might want to include that

60
00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:57.800
But even there if you can avoid it that's a good thing

61
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.800
So when talking about a module the kind of minimum thing that a module needs to be

62
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.800
How it's defined is three things really

63
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.800
We have the working directory

64
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.800
So typically in the engine this would be the most common case for modules that you would already see inside Unreal

65
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:14.800
Or inside source or runtime

66
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.800
I'll just quickly jump to that example

67
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.800
When you're in an Unreal C++ project you can see we've got it split up between our engine here and our game

68
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.800
And if we go to our source and runtime section

69
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.800
We have a few different sections but the one with the most in it is our runtime

70
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.800
And you can see each of these is a module

71
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.800
So they have their own working directory

72
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.800
If we were just to open up application core

73
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.800
You can see this is clearly a module because it's got its own buildcs file

74
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:39.800
Which is another component

75
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.800
And then it's got all the source files for that module inside there

76
00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.800
So we have the actual working directory so a folder to contain our module

77
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.800
Then we have the buildcs which I just quickly showed before

78
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.800
Which we'll go into a little bit more detail in a second

79
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:57.800
And then finally we have just a class, a cpp file that actually represents the module itself

80
00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.800
Where we can implement that module

81
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.370
So looking a bit more at the buildcs file

82
00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:09.589
This has a couple of different things we can introduce into it

83
00:05:09.589 --> 00:05:18.589
But most importantly is these two arrays of public dependency module names and private dependency module names

84
00:05:18.589 --> 00:05:21.589
We can look at our primary gameplay module here

85
00:05:21.589 --> 00:05:24.589
And if we jump inside here we can see public dependency module names

86
00:05:24.589 --> 00:05:28.589
This is by default whenever you make a new in Unreal Engine project

87
00:05:28.589 --> 00:05:31.589
It will already have a primary gameplay module

88
00:05:31.589 --> 00:05:36.589
It will already have a buildcs and this buildcs will come with these four modules being relied on

89
00:05:36.589 --> 00:05:41.589
It will be very very difficult to create an Unreal Engine game not using these

90
00:05:41.589 --> 00:05:43.589
So they're just in there by default

91
00:05:43.589 --> 00:05:48.589
And so we have the public dependency module names and private dependency module names

92
00:05:48.589 --> 00:05:52.589
The distinction between these two is a little bit important

93
00:05:52.589 --> 00:05:59.589
Essentially it means that a public dependency module name is where anything that your module openly relies on

94
00:05:59.589 --> 00:06:05.589
So when other modules are including this one these are modules that those modules will know you rely on

95
00:06:05.589 --> 00:06:10.589
Private dependency module names are really what we should be trying to use as much as possible

96
00:06:10.589 --> 00:06:14.589
It's not exposing our use of those modules in the same way

97
00:06:14.589 --> 00:06:22.589
It's a little bit more something you'd be needing to rely on when you're doing more engine based modules

98
00:06:22.589 --> 00:06:27.589
You'll see that typically within your gameplay module you don't have a division

99
00:06:27.589 --> 00:06:30.589
Unreal doesn't create an automatic division of public and private here

100
00:06:30.589 --> 00:06:35.589
It treats most of the primary gameplay modules as private by default

101
00:06:35.589 --> 00:06:40.589
But if you look towards any module inside Unreal Engine's code base itself

102
00:06:40.589 --> 00:06:46.069
You'll see you'll get this public and private division here

103
00:06:46.069 --> 00:06:52.069
And that's when your private dependency module names and your public dependency module names become a little bit more important

104
00:06:52.069 --> 00:06:56.069
But just generally I suppose the process should be

105
00:06:56.069 --> 00:07:00.069
Try to use private as much as you can unless something needs to be public

106
00:07:00.069 --> 00:07:07.300
So when it actually comes to creating a module

107
00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:12.300
We can't just put the build CS in, put our module CPP in

108
00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:17.300
And expect the code to get detected and compiled and linked

109
00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:23.300
Otherwise that would be the same as saying we're building and compiling all Unreal Engine's code all the time

110
00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:24.300
Which we obviously don't want to do

111
00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:30.300
So there's a couple of steps beyond just making a module to actually connect it to our game

112
00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:35.360
First and foremost we need to actually link it to our primary gameplay module

113
00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.360
This is the most common way of doing it

114
00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.360
Or any module that our primary gameplay module is using

115
00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:45.360
So as we can see here again in our primary gameplay module's build CS

116
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.360
We can just add that to a dependent module

117
00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:54.360
So our core, core U object, engine and input core here are going to be getting compiled and then linked

118
00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:58.360
Any modules that aren't included there the compiler isn't even going to think about

119
00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:02.360
Because we're essentially saying I'm not trying to use these for this project

120
00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:07.360
The other thing that we can do is add our modules to a target CS

121
00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:13.459
This is slightly less common but it's really important and powerful and it's an important thing to understand

122
00:08:13.459 --> 00:08:17.459
So whenever you make an Unreal Engine project for the first time

123
00:08:17.459 --> 00:08:23.459
You'll come with your primary gameplay module and you'll come with two of these target CS files

124
00:08:23.459 --> 00:08:29.459
These essentially correlate with the different build targets that we can have

125
00:08:29.459 --> 00:08:37.460
So our example55project.targetcs is used whenever we do debug game, development or shipping

126
00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:44.460
And our 55projecteditor.targetcs is used whenever we're doing an editor target

127
00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:49.129
It covers our editor targets, so debug game editor and development editor

128
00:08:49.129 --> 00:08:56.129
So essentially these target CSs let us highlight modules that we want to use when we're building that particular target

129
00:08:56.129 --> 00:09:02.129
So when we're creating our normal project target we obviously need to include the primary gameplay module

130
00:09:02.129 --> 00:09:08.129
But when we're building for editor we can additionally include editor only targets

131
00:09:08.129 --> 00:09:12.129
So this wouldn't come in by default, this is something I've actually made myself

132
00:09:12.129 --> 00:09:17.129
These two target CSs are in there by default, but I've additionally just to kind of demonstrate this

133
00:09:17.129 --> 00:09:25.129
Added an editor module that would, in a normal gameplay scenario you might add in some developer tools

134
00:09:25.129 --> 00:09:27.129
Some additional editor functionality

135
00:09:27.129 --> 00:09:32.129
So I've added this 55projecteditor, I've included that in our module names

136
00:09:32.129 --> 00:09:36.129
So that when we're building the editor target we want to include this module

137
00:09:36.129 --> 00:09:43.129
And then if we quickly glimpse into this module buildcs we can see I've added my 55project as a public dependency

138
00:09:43.129 --> 00:09:46.129
Again, something we should be trying to avoid when we can

139
00:09:46.129 --> 00:09:52.129
But I'm just imagining that our editor, it's quite likely we would need access to particular actors we've defined inside our gameplay module

140
00:09:52.129 --> 00:09:53.129
Or something like that

141
00:09:53.129 --> 00:09:58.129
But for the most part you should be trying to avoid this so that we don't end up with circular dependencies

142
00:09:58.129 --> 00:10:03.129
But because our primary gameplay module doesn't depend on our editor module

143
00:10:03.129 --> 00:10:05.129
That's not going to be likely

144
00:10:05.129 --> 00:10:08.129
And then we've got our CPP

145
00:10:08.129 --> 00:10:12.129
And this is where we actually implement the module ourselves

146
00:10:12.129 --> 00:10:18.129
Now the other element that gets included and should be borne in mind is the fact that we have

147
00:10:18.129 --> 00:10:21.159
Whenever we're creating an editor module

148
00:10:21.159 --> 00:10:28.159
The way you do that is we have something, we need a class that inherits from a module interface

149
00:10:28.159 --> 00:10:34.159
And that brings with it the functionality to highlight this startup and shutdown module

150
00:10:34.159 --> 00:10:37.159
By default most modules you see in Unreal Engine will have this

151
00:10:37.159 --> 00:10:42.159
They'll have a module header and it inherits from module interface

152
00:10:42.159 --> 00:10:44.159
You don't actually need to do that

153
00:10:44.159 --> 00:10:47.159
If you want a very very minimal implementation of a module

154
00:10:47.159 --> 00:10:49.159
You can actually only have the CPP

155
00:10:49.159 --> 00:10:54.159
And you can use this minimal implementation here where you say implement module

156
00:10:54.159 --> 00:10:57.159
And you just use this F default module implementation

157
00:10:57.159 --> 00:11:05.159
Where if we jump to that you can see that's just an empty class that inherits from iModule interface in the module manager header

158
00:11:05.159 --> 00:11:11.159
So if you really really don't need much control over what happens on startup and shutdown of your module

159
00:11:11.159 --> 00:11:12.159
You can use this

160
00:11:12.159 --> 00:11:14.159
But the vast majority of the time you would have

161
00:11:14.159 --> 00:11:19.159
You would define a module class, inherit from your module interface

162
00:11:19.159 --> 00:11:24.159
And then that gives you access to a startup module and a shutdown module functionality

163
00:11:24.159 --> 00:11:29.340
So once we've actually created the module

164
00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:34.340
The next step that we need to do is talk about how is this module used by our project

165
00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:37.340
When does it get loaded and what type of module is it

166
00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:42.340
The place that you define these is inside your project's UProject file

167
00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:44.340
So I'll just quickly jump to that and show

168
00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:47.340
But we'll come back to this in a second in a bit more detail

169
00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:52.340
But if I just go to our example 5.5 project

170
00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.340
You can see I've got my UProject file here

171
00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:57.340
This is actually just a JSON under the hood

172
00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:02.340
So if I open this up with either Notepad++ or any text editing software really

173
00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:05.370
We can see this is what our UProject file looks like

174
00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:08.370
We've got an engine version here, we've got some description and category stuff

175
00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:12.370
But the kind of key thing we're talking about here is this modules array

176
00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:14.370
This is what we really need to worry about here

177
00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.370
And you can see we've got our primary gameplay module

178
00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:21.370
The names here are important, they need to match up with the actual name we use for that module

179
00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.370
And then we define a type for that module

180
00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:25.370
So this is a runtime one

181
00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:29.370
And we've got our editor module here which is editor and we define a load phase

182
00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:34.460
So a couple of the options we have available to us when we're talking about what type of module it is

183
00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.460
And when it loads

184
00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:40.460
The kind of most common ones would be an editor module

185
00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.460
Which again is loaded when we're doing an editor target

186
00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:45.460
A runtime module

187
00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:49.460
We also have developer tool which is similar to an editor target

188
00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:53.460
But it's tied particularly to something inside our target cs

189
00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.460
Which is this build developer tools bool

190
00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:02.460
When that's set to true that means that this module will get included

191
00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:07.460
That can be useful when you're doing something that you don't want to bring the whole editor with it

192
00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:12.460
But you do need some set of developer tools that you definitely don't want to go into the shipping game

193
00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:16.659
That's a case where it might not be an editor but it might be a developer tool

194
00:13:16.659 --> 00:13:19.659
And then we have server only and client only targets

195
00:13:19.659 --> 00:13:22.659
And that would be pretty self explanatory

196
00:13:22.659 --> 00:13:26.659
It's when you want sets of modules that would only go into the server or only into the client

197
00:13:26.659 --> 00:13:29.659
An example might be you might have all your UI in a module

198
00:13:29.659 --> 00:13:34.659
And then obviously your server doesn't need UI so you don't include any of that code

199
00:13:34.659 --> 00:13:37.940
The second thing is this load phases

200
00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:42.940
Again some typical examples would be just before the loading screen of the game

201
00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:46.940
The default loading phase which is kind of in the middle of the engine booting up

202
00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.940
And then post engine in it

203
00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:54.940
Post engine in it is very very common for editor because you want your entire engine to be loaded in

204
00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:57.940
Before you start injecting additional functionality on top

205
00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:03.129
So again coming back to this is just another preview of a U project

206
00:14:03.129 --> 00:14:05.129
We've got the name of the modules we're using

207
00:14:05.129 --> 00:14:09.129
This type is some examples we just ran through

208
00:14:09.129 --> 00:14:12.129
And our loading phase again some examples we just ran through

209
00:14:12.129 --> 00:14:14.129
This isn't a full comprehensive list

210
00:14:14.129 --> 00:14:18.129
If you actually wanted to see all of those you can go to the

211
00:14:21.259 --> 00:14:23.259
If I just quickly jump to the file

212
00:14:23.259 --> 00:14:25.350
It's module descriptor dot h

213
00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:29.350
And in here we have a full list of all the loading phases

214
00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:34.379
And all the host types which is our module types

215
00:14:34.379 --> 00:14:41.379
So if you want to see all the options available to you, you can just jump into the module description header to take a look at those

216
00:14:41.379 --> 00:14:49.139
The other thing to highlight is that modules aren't just to be completely independent of your code base

217
00:14:49.139 --> 00:14:52.139
Sometimes it's not just injecting functionality

218
00:14:52.139 --> 00:14:56.139
But once you've included that module as something that your module depends on

219
00:14:56.139 --> 00:14:58.139
You want actual access to that module

220
00:14:58.139 --> 00:15:02.139
And modules can be accessed relatively easily through this function

221
00:15:02.139 --> 00:15:05.139
The fmodule manager and load module checked

222
00:15:05.139 --> 00:15:09.139
So a typical example and one that I've personally used quite a lot

223
00:15:09.139 --> 00:15:11.139
Is the asset registry module from the engine

224
00:15:11.139 --> 00:15:14.139
So the way you might access that is through this function

225
00:15:14.139 --> 00:15:16.139
fmodule manager load module checked

226
00:15:16.139 --> 00:15:20.139
Then we use the module class type

227
00:15:20.139 --> 00:15:23.139
So in this case fasset registry module

228
00:15:23.139 --> 00:15:25.139
And then we provide this string here

229
00:15:25.139 --> 00:15:27.139
This isn't just an internal name that we give to it

230
00:15:27.139 --> 00:15:33.139
This tallies with what we use in our implement module macro

231
00:15:33.139 --> 00:15:37.139
Which is typically done inside your module cpp

232
00:15:37.139 --> 00:15:40.139
So in the case of our editor, it's this second one here

233
00:15:40.139 --> 00:15:44.139
It's implement module is this important macro to say this module exists

234
00:15:44.139 --> 00:15:49.139
You say which class it's using and here's the name you then refer to this module by

235
00:15:49.139 --> 00:15:52.139
So example 5.5 project editor

236
00:15:52.139 --> 00:15:54.139
In the case of our game, it's the same thing

237
00:15:54.139 --> 00:15:56.139
Our primary gameplay module

238
00:15:56.139 --> 00:15:59.139
If we go to our primary gameplay module cpp

239
00:15:59.139 --> 00:16:03.139
We use this implement primary game module macro

240
00:16:03.139 --> 00:16:05.139
And it's this term here

241
00:16:05.139 --> 00:16:08.139
If we for some reason needed to access our primary gameplay module

242
00:16:08.139 --> 00:16:10.139
It would be using this term here

243
00:16:10.139 --> 00:16:13.139
So that is what you need to provide in this string here

244
00:16:13.139 --> 00:16:15.139
If you want to access that module

245
00:16:15.139 --> 00:16:22.139
What this means is that you can essentially create a module that is just a library of functionality

246
00:16:22.139 --> 00:16:25.139
If you just needed some extra functions to

247
00:16:25.139 --> 00:16:31.179
For example, download some files or provide some http functionality or something like that

248
00:16:31.179 --> 00:16:35.179
Your module doesn't need to bring in a whole lot of different things

249
00:16:35.179 --> 00:16:36.179
A whole load of different classes

250
00:16:36.179 --> 00:16:40.179
It could literally just be a single cpp file with some helper functions in it

251
00:16:40.179 --> 00:16:43.179
And then you could access that through this load module checked

252
00:16:43.179 --> 00:16:45.909
Moving on from modules

253
00:16:45.909 --> 00:16:48.909
But still thinking about that kind of portability that they bring

254
00:16:48.909 --> 00:16:52.909
We can zoom out a little bit and look at plugins

255
00:16:52.909 --> 00:16:57.909
Plugins are similar to modules in that they are separated from our initial project

256
00:16:57.909 --> 00:17:00.909
But they bring a couple of extra features to them

257
00:17:00.909 --> 00:17:04.910
So one of the key things that a plugin has to it

258
00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:07.910
Is it can be made up of multiple modules

259
00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:08.910
If we think about something like

260
00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:14.910
Imagine I've got some advanced 3D pathfinding functionality

261
00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:17.910
So I've got something like a navmesh but it's a 3D navmesh

262
00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:20.910
Then it's clearly going to have a runtime and an editor component

263
00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:23.910
It's going to have the actual runtime navmesh

264
00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:27.910
But when I'm interacting with an editor I want some editor functionality

265
00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:31.910
So that would be a case where that plugin would have two modules

266
00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:35.910
We would have the kind of core module that provides that 3D navmesh

267
00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:37.910
And then we would have an editor module

268
00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:42.910
Because we don't want all of this editor functionality getting exported into the shipping build of our game

269
00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:44.910
So we need multiple modules

270
00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:47.910
If that's the case, a plugin is definitely something that you should be considering

271
00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.910
And it brings all that extra capability

272
00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:53.910
The other thing about advantage of plugins

273
00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:56.910
Is that it's got a very nice editor implementation

274
00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:00.910
You can just very easily look up all the plugins available to you

275
00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:02.910
Through your unreal engine

276
00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:03.910
Through the edit plugins tab

277
00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:05.910
I'll just quickly demonstrate that actually

278
00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:08.910
If I jump to our example project

279
00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:10.910
Just hit edit plugins

280
00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:13.910
And you can see all the plugins available to you

281
00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:16.910
And you'll see a bunch of them come in enabled by default

282
00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:19.910
It's actually worth just taking a little look through this in your own time

283
00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:23.910
Because there's whole sets of features here that you might never know existed

284
00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:25.910
Until you start browsing through this

285
00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:30.420
But you can see these are the built-in plugins that come with the engine

286
00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:34.420
Again, not all of these or a lot of these in fact won't be enabled by default

287
00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:36.420
So it's worth looking through them

288
00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:40.420
But you can also install plugins to your engine for additional functionality

289
00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:50.140
So those would be listed here

290
00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:54.140
The last thing about taking this kind of portability idea further

291
00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.140
Is that when you make a plugin

292
00:18:56.140 --> 00:19:01.140
It makes it very easy to not just bring it from project to project internally

293
00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:04.140
But it also means you can put it on the marketplace

294
00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:06.140
Either for money or for free

295
00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:11.140
So Fab is the kind of new marketplace for not just unreal stuff

296
00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:15.140
But also I think they have unity and other software on there

297
00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:18.140
And yes, basically turning something into a plugin

298
00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:20.140
Makes it very easy to host up here

299
00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:24.140
And also it's worth bearing in mind that when you're thinking about a new set of features

300
00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:27.140
It's possible that someone already has provided that on Fab

301
00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:29.140
And that might just save you a lot of development time

302
00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.140
So just something worth bearing in mind when you're considering plugins

303
00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:33.140
That the Fab marketplace is there

304
00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:35.140
You might be able to put that plugin up there

305
00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:38.259
Or find something and save some time

306
00:19:38.259 --> 00:19:40.259
So when actually talking about creating a plugin

307
00:19:40.259 --> 00:19:42.259
It's very very simple

308
00:19:42.259 --> 00:19:45.259
If you want to make your new one from scratch

309
00:19:45.259 --> 00:19:49.259
The easiest way is just to go to your plugins window

310
00:19:49.259 --> 00:19:52.259
And you can see you've just got this add button in the top left here

311
00:19:52.259 --> 00:19:54.450
If we open that up

312
00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:57.450
We've got a couple of options here for what kind of plugin this is going to be

313
00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:59.450
You can say where you want that to go

314
00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:01.450
And then you can just define some basic information about it

315
00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:03.450
About who's authoring this

316
00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:04.450
What's the description of this plugin

317
00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:06.450
And once you go to create plugin

318
00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:08.450
It will go through a process that will take a couple of minutes

319
00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:09.450
And it will create a new one

320
00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:11.450
But then you'll get something

321
00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:13.450
Your plugin will be created for you

322
00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:15.450
And you'll get something a little bit like this

323
00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:18.960
So your plugins don't go into your source folder

324
00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:21.960
They actually live alongside your project in their own folder

325
00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:24.960
When you create a new Unreal project for the first time

326
00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:26.960
This plugin folder won't exist

327
00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:29.960
Until you try creating a plugin for that project

328
00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:31.960
If you want to pull some plugins in

329
00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:33.960
You can just make this folder yourself

330
00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:35.960
But this will get automatically generated

331
00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:37.960
So this is an example plugin that I made

332
00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:39.960
It has no content really

333
00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:41.960
But it's just to kind of show you the breakdown of it

334
00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:45.960
You'll notice that it looks quite a lot like our actual project

335
00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:48.960
It's got its own source folder

336
00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:50.960
It's got its own content folder

337
00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:52.960
It's got an intermediate folder, a binaries folder

338
00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:55.960
The distinction is you know a plugin when you see it

339
00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:57.960
Because it doesn't have a U project file

340
00:20:57.960 --> 00:20:59.960
It has a U plugin file

341
00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:02.059
The other thing to highlight as well

342
00:21:02.059 --> 00:21:04.059
Is it's got this resources folder

343
00:21:04.059 --> 00:21:06.059
By default this will just have this one thing in it

344
00:21:06.059 --> 00:21:08.059
Which is the icon

345
00:21:08.059 --> 00:21:10.059
And this is essentially what's displayed in engine

346
00:21:10.059 --> 00:21:13.059
So if we actually go and look

347
00:21:13.059 --> 00:21:17.009
You can see my example plugin

348
00:21:17.009 --> 00:21:19.009
This is the icon

349
00:21:19.009 --> 00:21:21.009
If I were to replace this

350
00:21:21.009 --> 00:21:23.009
If I were to replace the icon here

351
00:21:23.009 --> 00:21:25.009
That's what would come up on the editor

352
00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:31.220
So if we just quickly look at the plugin file

353
00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:34.220
We can see very very similar to our U project

354
00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:36.220
Some file versioning

355
00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:38.220
What version this plugin actually is

356
00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:40.220
If this is going to go up in the marketplace

357
00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:42.220
It would have a URL, some support information

358
00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:44.220
And then what modules this plugin uses

359
00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:46.220
So very much like the U project

360
00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:48.220
A plugin can have multiple modules to it

361
00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:51.220
And this works in exactly the same way as the U project

